--TEST--
a `throw` arm produces no value, so the failure leaves through the enclosing region and runs its `finally` on the way out — ADR 0119 §§ 2, 3 and 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0119 § 3: the arm body is an expression, and `throw expr` is already
// one, so an arm may wrap and rethrow with no rule of its own. It is typed
// `never` (§ 4), which is why it joins neither the union nor the phi § 6
// builds — the arm has no edge into the join at all.
//
// § 2: `finally` stays the block form's alone, so the region that runs one
// here is the `try` around the whole expression.
class Fail {
    public static function io(int $n): int {
        if ($n < 0) {
            throw new IOError("io");
        }
        return $n;
    }
}

try {
    try {
        echo Fail::io(-1) catch (IOError $e) => throw new LogicError("wrapped: " . $e->message), "\n";
        echo "not reached\n";
    } finally {
        echo "the inner finally ran\n";
    }
} catch (LogicError $wrapped) {
    echo $wrapped->message, "\n";
}

// The arm contributing nothing to the union is observable in the type: the
// whole expression is the guard's own `int`, not a union with a `never` in it.
int $n = Fail::io(4) catch (IOError $rethrow) => throw $rethrow;
echo $n, "\n";
--EXPECT--
the inner finally ran
wrapped: io
4
